Let $\nu$ be a complex measure on $(X, M)$, and let $|\nu|$ denote its total variation measure. We are given that $\nu(X) = |\nu|(X)$, and we must show $\nu = |\nu|$.

**Polar decomposition.** By the Radon–Nikodym theorem applied to $\nu \ll |\nu|$, there exists a measurable function $h : X \to \mathbb{C}$ with $|h(x)| = 1$ for $|\nu|$-a.e. $x$ such that
$$d\nu = h\,d|\nu|.$$
Consequently, for every $E \in M$,
$$\nu(E) = \int_E h\,d|\nu|, \qquad |\nu|(E) = \int_E 1\,d|\nu|.$$

**Using the hypothesis.** The hypothesis $\nu(X) = |\nu|(X)$ reads
$$\int_X h\,d|\nu| = \int_X 1\,d|\nu| = |\nu|(X). \tag{$\ast$}$$

**Equality case of the triangle inequality.** In general,
$$\left|\int_X h\,d|\nu|\right| \le \int_X |h|\,d|\nu| = \int_X 1\,d|\nu| = |\nu|(X),$$
since $|h|=1$ $|\nu|$-a.e. By $(\ast)$, the left-hand side equals $|\nu|(X)$, which is a nonnegative real number. Thus equality holds in
$$\left|\int_X h\,d|\nu|\right| \le \int_X |h|\,d|\nu|.$$

Equality in this integral triangle inequality for a complex-valued integrand occurs if and only if $h$ has a constant argument $|\nu|$-a.e.; that is, there exists $\theta \in \mathbb{R}$ such that
$$h(x) = e^{i\theta} \quad \text{for } |\nu|\text{-a.e. } x.$$

**Determining the phase.** Substituting back into $(\ast)$,
$$e^{i\theta}\,|\nu|(X) = |\nu|(X).$$
If $|\nu|(X) = 0$, then $\nu \equiv 0 = |\nu|$ and we are done. Otherwise $|\nu|(X) > 0$, so dividing gives $e^{i\theta} = 1$, hence $\theta \equiv 0 \pmod{2\pi}$ and
$$h(x) = 1 \quad \text{for } |\nu|\text{-a.e. } x.$$

**Conclusion.** Since $h = 1$ $|\nu|$-a.e.,
$$\nu(E) = \int_E h\,d|\nu| = \int_E 1\,d|\nu| = |\nu|(E) \quad \text{for every } E \in M.$$
Therefore $\nu = |\nu|$ as measures on $(X, M)$. $\blacksquare$

